A primitive datatype, also known as a basic data Type or scalar dataType, is a data variable that can directly store a single value. These dataTypes are built into the C or C++ programming language. Some examples of Primitive DataTypes include:
	+ int a = 10;
 + float b =3.14;	 or
 + double c =2.71828; // double is an extended precision floating- point data
*- char (character) 
 	 	 + char d ='A';
In C and C ++, the following are the main primitive Data Types: 
2 char(character): A single character is stored in this data-type. It is represented using single quotes. For example, 'A' or 'B'.
3 float(real number): To store real numbers, we use the float data -type or double data – type. float can represent real values with a precision of 7 decimal digits. The size of float is 32 bits or around 5 bytes. double can handle larger real number values and has a size around of around double the size (64 bits).
4 bool(Boolean): Boolean data–type represents logical values i.e. either true or false. C does not have a Boolean Data Type. Instead, it uses integers. 0 represents false, any non-zero value represents true. In CPP, bool is introduced in Cpp11, representing only two logical states. bool b; b=true; or b==false. However, in some compilers, like MS Visual Studio, you can define it as below: enum Boolean { False, True };
5 void: void is not a variable but a keyword, used as function return type when a function doesnot return anything. void main();
